1
00:00:01,232 --> 00:00:16,084
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how would, say, a householder, you know, with his life and duties and so on, would they be able to practice mindfulness of the body rightly?

2
00:00:16,184 --> 00:00:21,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, according to you, how would you explain to them what mindfulness of the body is?

3
00:00:22,689 --> 00:00:27,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they can practice it if they fulfill certain requirements that will

4
00:00:28,191 --> 00:00:31,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Essentially, undo all the things that cover up the body for them.

5
00:00:32,794 --> 00:00:41,618
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha was only teaching mindfulness of the body to, like, 99% of the time to monks, and whenever he taught it to lay people, it's people who already had the right view.

6
00:00:42,439 --> 00:00:54,085
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to be able to see that body, you have to be emulating those principles that will stop covering up the body.

7
00:00:54,325 --> 00:00:56,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because there was... there were suttas where

8
00:00:57,908 --> 00:01:01,330
[SPEAKER_01]: It was said that four satipaṭṭhānas could be used to develop the right view.

9
00:01:01,370 --> 00:01:01,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

10
00:01:01,990 --> 00:01:16,357
[SPEAKER_00]: But if those suttas were taught to monks, so people who were established in a certain conduct, and that conduct, the relevance of that conduct is, it is what allows the body to be discerned.

11
00:01:16,837 --> 00:01:17,957
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't cover it up anymore.

12
00:01:18,978 --> 00:01:21,039
[SPEAKER_00]: So what's covering it up?

13
00:01:21,079 --> 00:01:25,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, action is rooted in greed and aversion, not keeping the precepts.

14
00:01:27,470 --> 00:01:31,934
[SPEAKER_01]: So, acting out against the precepts covers up the body.

15
00:01:31,954 --> 00:01:32,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

16
00:01:32,694 --> 00:01:37,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you make all the effort in the world to see it within your breaking of the precepts, that...

17
00:01:37,759 --> 00:01:38,940
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't be the body you're looking at.

18
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:39,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

19
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:41,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Those actions cover it up simply by being done.

20
00:01:41,822 --> 00:01:47,867
[SPEAKER_01]: And, say, if you are not doing those actions, but you are not making the effort to see it, would that uncover it?

21
00:01:49,088 --> 00:01:56,074
[SPEAKER_01]: If you truly are disengaged from all of those actions, even by mind, then, yes, the body would be uncovered.

22
00:01:57,136 --> 00:01:59,123
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is... how do I disengage?

23
00:01:59,143 --> 00:02:02,054
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm keeping the presets, but how do I disengage my mind?

24
00:02:03,318 --> 00:02:03,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's...

25
00:02:05,565 --> 00:02:15,734
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... you want to see not just specific directions of action, but anything where a certain... What do you mean specific directions?

26
00:02:15,994 --> 00:02:17,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Speak in layman's terms, just practically.

27
00:02:18,056 --> 00:02:26,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the precepts, you don't want to just think, oh, okay, I'm not killing, I'm not stealing, I'm celibate, and all this, and then everything else is fine.

28
00:02:28,765 --> 00:02:31,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else not defined by the precepts is fine.

29
00:02:31,267 --> 00:02:32,388
[SPEAKER_01]: I shouldn't be seeing that.

30
00:02:32,448 --> 00:02:33,269
[SPEAKER_01]: I shouldn't be thinking that.

31
00:02:33,349 --> 00:02:34,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you shouldn't be thinking that.

32
00:02:34,630 --> 00:02:36,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Just because it's not against the precepts, it's fine.

33
00:02:36,391 --> 00:02:36,852
[SPEAKER_00]: It's fine.

34
00:02:36,952 --> 00:02:37,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Uh-huh.

35
00:02:37,272 --> 00:02:40,215
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what then constitutes fine and not fine?

36
00:02:40,235 --> 00:02:45,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you want to see what's your intention or your mental state behind doing it or attending to it.

37
00:02:45,859 --> 00:02:46,299
[SPEAKER_01]: Examples?

38
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:48,041
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's say,

39
00:02:49,766 --> 00:03:16,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Take somebody who is established in the precepts, but then is doing one of these breath-focusing absorption practices that are very popular today, and they don't realize that that very attempt to attend and concentrate and focus is based on a certain mental pressure that mirrors exactly the same principle of sensuality and like the precepts that

40
00:03:16,948 --> 00:03:24,251
[SPEAKER_01]: But what if somebody says, well, I have no pressure to do it, doing it, you know, because it's neutral, pleasant, calming...

41
00:03:24,731 --> 00:03:25,672
[SPEAKER_00]: But what happens if you don't do it?

42
00:03:25,692 --> 00:03:26,592
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the problem with that?

43
00:03:27,092 --> 00:03:28,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, nothing happens if I don't do it.

44
00:03:30,294 --> 00:03:31,054
[SPEAKER_01]: But I choose to do it.

45
00:03:31,114 --> 00:03:32,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there a problem in doing it neutrally?

46
00:03:32,695 --> 00:03:39,978
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem still is, even if it's neutral, that's not the place where you will find the body, because the body is not at the level of sensations.

47
00:03:41,781 --> 00:03:55,124
[SPEAKER_01]: So, say, the sensations, the internal sensations of the breath, say, the whole kind of sense of breathing that I'm attending to, is not the body.

48
00:03:55,144 --> 00:04:02,706
[SPEAKER_00]: No, those are just tactile sensations and there's no... they don't have any special significance just because of the position where they are.

49
00:04:06,067 --> 00:04:09,608
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, if attending to your breath

50
00:04:10,766 --> 00:04:18,850
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mind is not where the body is, why did the Buddha teach the mindfulness of breathing in order to develop the mindfulness of the body?

51
00:04:20,071 --> 00:04:27,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because the Buddha wasn't... nowhere in the ānāpānasati descriptions does it talk about sensations.

52
00:04:28,336 --> 00:04:31,537
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't talk about... it doesn't even talk about breathing as something that you observe.

53
00:04:32,878 --> 00:04:35,600
[SPEAKER_00]: It talks about it as an action, something that you train in.

54
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:39,942
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about... at no point does it say that it's about attending.

55
00:04:41,108 --> 00:04:42,088
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is it about then?

56
00:04:42,768 --> 00:04:52,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's about maintaining the perspective of the actual body, that's the basis for your attention, while you're acting through breathing.

57
00:04:55,351 --> 00:04:59,411
[SPEAKER_01]: So, maintaining the perspective of the actual body, what is the actual body then?

58
00:05:00,492 --> 00:05:07,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, once that actual body has been... it's that basis for your entire

59
00:05:10,834 --> 00:05:12,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, that's just getting too abstract now.

60
00:05:12,655 --> 00:05:12,815
[SPEAKER_00]: It's...

61
00:05:14,116 --> 00:05:17,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically speaking, I want to be mindful of the body.

62
00:05:17,498 --> 00:05:19,759
[SPEAKER_01]: I read the mindfulness of the breathing sutta.

63
00:05:19,979 --> 00:05:23,901
[SPEAKER_01]: You're telling me I shouldn't be attending to breath, should know the basis or something.

64
00:05:23,921 --> 00:05:25,582
[SPEAKER_01]: But, like, what...

65
00:05:25,602 --> 00:05:26,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I'm not attending to the breath.

66
00:05:26,983 --> 00:05:28,824
[SPEAKER_01]: What do I do to see that body?

67
00:05:29,705 --> 00:05:30,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's not something that you do.

68
00:05:31,946 --> 00:05:35,808
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a positive object that you can clarify through some effort.

69
00:05:36,128 --> 00:05:37,569
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something that has to be uncovered.

70
00:05:37,793 --> 00:05:39,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so what... how do I uncover it?

71
00:05:40,514 --> 00:05:45,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, having assumed that you're then establishing the precepts, as we said...

72
00:05:45,077 --> 00:05:46,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I take on the precepts.

73
00:05:46,437 --> 00:05:47,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

74
00:05:47,458 --> 00:05:53,281
[SPEAKER_01]: I take on, as you said, just because it's not against the precept doesn't mean it's not an obstruction.

75
00:05:53,841 --> 00:06:01,685
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I recognize the intentions, the pressures, the compulsions, even towards seemingly good things, but I don't do it because it's rooted in the wrong things.

76
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:04,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything else?

77
00:06:04,440 --> 00:06:09,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Or would that mean, like, I'm now seeing the body correctly?

78
00:06:09,061 --> 00:06:10,482
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're established in that, yes.

79
00:06:12,302 --> 00:06:13,782
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if I don't make the effort to see it?

80
00:06:15,383 --> 00:06:19,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you have to make effort to not do the things that will cover it up.

81
00:06:19,964 --> 00:06:21,664
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to make effort to see it.

82
00:06:21,684 --> 00:06:21,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

83
00:06:22,104 --> 00:06:23,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, that's what I'm asking.

84
00:06:23,545 --> 00:06:32,787
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, if I'm established in these modes of behavior rightly, not just keep the precepts in that external sense, and

85
00:06:35,418 --> 00:06:36,258
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, I have a...

86
00:06:36,398 --> 00:06:53,666
[SPEAKER_01]: I have a... because I've been, like, say, following what you say, listening to talks, reading the writings, I'm keeping the precepts, practicing sense of shame, but I also have, obviously, a strong urge, hope, that that will reveal the body to me.

87
00:06:55,586 --> 00:06:57,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, if you... Is that fine?

88
00:06:58,428 --> 00:06:59,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Acting out of it is not fine.

89
00:07:00,428 --> 00:07:01,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting out of that strong...

90
00:07:01,709 --> 00:07:02,789
[SPEAKER_00]: Compulsion, yeah.

91
00:07:02,849 --> 00:07:03,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Hope.

92
00:07:03,150 --> 00:07:04,110
[SPEAKER_00]: To find the body, yeah.

93
00:07:04,997 --> 00:07:08,538
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to not deny it and try to get rid of it, but you have to see...

94
00:07:08,578 --> 00:07:19,223
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I'm doing all the practices you described, and I can't help... can't help but hope that that's going to reveal the true body to me, I shouldn't be acting out of that either.

95
00:07:19,483 --> 00:07:19,643
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

96
00:07:20,303 --> 00:07:25,765
[SPEAKER_00]: You should include it just as you were including all the other things, like pressure to break the precepts arose.

97
00:07:25,965 --> 00:07:26,766
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I include it?

98
00:07:27,786 --> 00:07:32,248
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I include my hope to see the... because, you know, it's like a factual goal I'm striving to,

99
00:07:33,404 --> 00:07:38,650
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do I include that goal of seeing the body correctly and not falling into a trap?

100
00:07:39,070 --> 00:07:39,871
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I include it?

101
00:07:40,252 --> 00:07:41,093
[SPEAKER_01]: By not acting out of it.

102
00:07:41,954 --> 00:07:44,016
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I act out of it?

103
00:07:46,138 --> 00:07:51,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking for granted and starting to do things out of that hope.

104
00:07:53,346 --> 00:07:53,607
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

105
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:58,163
[SPEAKER_01]: The hope that I feel towards that body being revealed to me...

106
00:07:58,203 --> 00:08:04,847
[SPEAKER_00]: Trying to clarify it, trying to see it more, trying to rehash your ideas about it, all of that, you're taking up that hope.

107
00:08:04,907 --> 00:08:05,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Aha!

108
00:08:05,187 --> 00:08:11,692
[SPEAKER_01]: So, intentionally choosing to sort of mentally kind of push down further.

109
00:08:11,752 --> 00:08:12,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

110
00:08:13,233 --> 00:08:17,596
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, if I say, okay, I don't do that now, is that enough?

111
00:08:17,636 --> 00:08:18,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I just... what do I do?

112
00:08:18,756 --> 00:08:20,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Just stay with that or...?

113
00:08:21,398 --> 00:08:22,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Well...

114
00:08:22,139 --> 00:08:23,960
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not acting on the body, not acting on speech,

115
00:08:24,403 --> 00:08:33,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognizing mental intentions and now the pressuring hope of seeing the body rightly, because, you know, ultimately I want to be free from suffering, I recognize that pressuring compulsion as a trap.

116
00:08:35,445 --> 00:08:37,005
[SPEAKER_01]: You mentioned I don't try to get rid of it.

117
00:08:39,166 --> 00:08:44,066
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't act out of it as in like, okay, I'm gonna rehash this and keep thinking about it so it gets quicker.

118
00:08:45,707 --> 00:08:46,567
[SPEAKER_01]: So I don't do that either.

119
00:08:47,527 --> 00:08:47,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Then what?

120
00:08:49,307 --> 00:08:52,908
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you need to protect that because other things will be coming up

121
00:08:53,284 --> 00:08:56,625
[SPEAKER_00]: That will be trying to absorb you out of pressure.

122
00:08:58,305 --> 00:08:59,165
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, okay.

123
00:08:59,385 --> 00:09:04,366
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, protecting it from being recovered, so to speak.

124
00:09:04,586 --> 00:09:04,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

125
00:09:05,106 --> 00:09:05,646
[SPEAKER_01]: Covered again.

126
00:09:05,886 --> 00:09:07,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not a once-and-done deal.

127
00:09:07,786 --> 00:09:19,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Other things, like, it's... it's... as the Buddha said in one sutta, it's like Mara trying to grab a footing through whichever sense object it can to get you to act out.

128
00:09:20,129 --> 00:09:22,309
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like you just, oh, you...

129
00:09:22,930 --> 00:09:25,031
[SPEAKER_00]: You did everything and you just sit down and forget about it.

130
00:09:25,271 --> 00:09:26,332
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something that you have to protect.

131
00:09:27,693 --> 00:09:40,561
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I'm... if I'm protecting it patiently, presumably for as long as it takes, can I then do the mindfulness of breathing?

132
00:09:41,822 --> 00:09:43,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I then do the ānāpānasati?

133
00:09:43,823 --> 00:09:51,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if it... if it's... if it's not, again, coming out of a special expectation,

134
00:09:53,103 --> 00:09:53,984
[SPEAKER_00]: That you have from it.

135
00:09:55,204 --> 00:10:01,407
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're just... No, I just want to further... push for... push further...

136
00:10:01,767 --> 00:10:08,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so if it's coming out of the expectation, it's... it's... it's a wrong practice.

137
00:10:08,510 --> 00:10:12,252
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the right form of ānāpānasati that the Buddha described.

138
00:10:13,692 --> 00:10:14,833
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do I do the right one?

139
00:10:16,894 --> 00:10:17,754
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are...

140
00:10:18,374 --> 00:10:19,655
[SPEAKER_01]: I do have expectation.

141
00:10:19,815 --> 00:10:19,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

142
00:10:21,240 --> 00:10:23,281
[SPEAKER_01]: So, does that mean I cannot do ānāpānasati?

143
00:10:24,021 --> 00:10:34,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you shouldn't do it with the expectation, like, as a... hoping, like, okay, sure, the expectation is there, but if I just do the practice, somehow, it will sort itself out.

144
00:10:34,726 --> 00:10:36,386
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you have to... Don't add on top of it.

145
00:10:36,426 --> 00:10:39,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the practice is in regard to that expectation, not like...

146
00:10:39,808 --> 00:10:46,491
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do I practice ānāpānasati in regard to the expectation that I have from ānāpānasati?

147
00:10:48,171 --> 00:10:49,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's...

148
00:10:50,771 --> 00:10:58,453
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I said before, you... the practice of ānāpānasati hinges on you seeing that body as...

149
00:10:58,473 --> 00:11:00,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, well, you could ask the same question for the body.

150
00:11:00,234 --> 00:11:05,115
[SPEAKER_01]: I still have expectation that the actual body, the real body, will be revealed.

151
00:11:05,135 --> 00:11:05,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

152
00:11:06,315 --> 00:11:17,018
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I practice the revealing of the actual body, protecting of the actual body, with expectations of seeing the actual body?

153
00:11:17,218 --> 00:11:19,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the thing is, expectations being there,

154
00:11:20,976 --> 00:11:25,059
[SPEAKER_00]: Expectations are risen and you acting out of them are two separate things.

155
00:11:25,099 --> 00:11:26,460
[SPEAKER_01]: So, okay, so I'm not acting out.

156
00:11:26,601 --> 00:11:26,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

157
00:11:27,201 --> 00:11:28,242
[SPEAKER_01]: But the expectation is there.

158
00:11:29,343 --> 00:11:34,626
[SPEAKER_01]: But anything I do on account of it, wouldn't that be... like back onto ānāpānasati.

159
00:11:34,666 --> 00:11:41,752
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I have the expectation that practice of ānāpānasati would lead to certain revelation, insights and so on.

160
00:11:43,012 --> 00:11:43,973
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not acting out of it.

161
00:11:45,774 --> 00:11:46,515
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm not doing it.

162
00:11:46,615 --> 00:11:49,217
[SPEAKER_01]: But does that mean I can't do ānāpānasati at all?

163
00:11:49,853 --> 00:11:57,780
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't approach mindfulness of the body through breath, because this expectation is in the way, because whatever I do would be kind of rooted in that expectation.

164
00:11:59,181 --> 00:12:00,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, but that's the thing.

165
00:12:01,523 --> 00:12:09,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Expectation can be there, but you taking it for granted, hoping to get the result of it, that doesn't follow automatically.

166
00:12:09,229 --> 00:12:10,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you see... That's not what I'm asking.

167
00:12:10,570 --> 00:12:12,072
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm not taking it for granted.

168
00:12:16,877 --> 00:12:23,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there any other way I could relate to breathing that would not be rooted in that expectation?

169
00:12:23,583 --> 00:12:29,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Or do I need to completely abandon any notion of practice of mindfulness of the body through breathing?

170
00:12:32,110 --> 00:12:44,140
[SPEAKER_01]: So, presumably, I've clarified it to that level through not acting out of body, speech, mind, watching my intentions, not taking for granted the desire to clarify the body, not acting out of that further passionately, containing it,

171
00:12:46,672 --> 00:12:48,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that ānāpānasati already?

172
00:12:48,974 --> 00:12:55,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Or can I do something, actually, that would not necessarily, automatically, be coming out of that expectation?

173
00:12:57,860 --> 00:13:03,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you could undermine the expectation, if you're seeing the body rightly.

174
00:13:03,603 --> 00:13:13,730
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yes, so, like we said, not acting out of the expectation, seeing the body already there, coming before it, and then breathing.

175
00:13:18,417 --> 00:13:24,342
[SPEAKER_01]: So, seeing the body coming before an expectation, that I have of seeing the body.

176
00:13:26,784 --> 00:13:27,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you explain that?

177
00:13:28,745 --> 00:13:37,512
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's... Well, if you haven't been acting out of the expectation, because, again, even if you take that up and then try to see the body, it's not going to work.

178
00:13:39,173 --> 00:13:40,214
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's there.

179
00:13:40,554 --> 00:13:47,220
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, what's revealed by you not taking the expectation for granted,

180
00:13:48,149 --> 00:13:56,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that kind of physical basis of, you could say, for example, your lungs being functional.

181
00:13:57,133 --> 00:14:00,234
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a theoretical idea, you have to clarify.

182
00:14:00,294 --> 00:14:06,696
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a factual just state.

183
00:14:08,217 --> 00:14:12,919
[SPEAKER_00]: That even if you were to overlook it, it would still be there and it's the entire reason that you're alive.

184
00:14:13,619 --> 00:14:15,900
[SPEAKER_00]: So you have to... you have to...

185
00:14:17,608 --> 00:14:36,281
[SPEAKER_00]: That will become clear, if you haven't been acting out of all these unwholesome things, and then maintaining the, let's say, peripheral sight of that fact, of functioning lungs, you could say, breathing simultaneously with that, without overemphasizing one or the other.

186
00:14:38,182 --> 00:14:39,243
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'm still doing the breathing.

187
00:14:40,204 --> 00:14:42,746
[SPEAKER_00]: You're doing it, but with this at the same time.

188
00:14:42,766 --> 00:14:46,008
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't let the breathing become the kind of

189
00:14:47,314 --> 00:14:48,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Fully focused, concentrated effort.

190
00:14:50,095 --> 00:14:50,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, okay.

191
00:14:50,595 --> 00:14:51,556
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to happen simultaneously.

192
00:14:51,576 --> 00:14:57,518
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not talking about necessarily, as I said, in the coarsest, misguided ways of meditation technique.

193
00:14:58,699 --> 00:15:10,904
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, the point is, like, is there a way that I can do the breathing rightly as a form of mindfulness of the body practice?

194
00:15:12,144 --> 00:15:15,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so, if you were balancing the two, so to speak,

195
00:15:16,927 --> 00:15:19,068
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not trying to over-clarify...

196
00:15:19,088 --> 00:15:22,190
[SPEAKER_01]: But, I mean, the sutta doesn't really say that you have to balance breathing with anything else.

197
00:15:24,952 --> 00:15:31,837
[SPEAKER_00]: At no point do I need to set prerequisites... Well, it says, mindfully, mindfully he breathes in, mindfully he breathes out.

198
00:15:32,117 --> 00:15:32,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

199
00:15:32,317 --> 00:15:40,442
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there has to be a knowledge while you're breathing, not just the breathing.

200
00:15:40,682 --> 00:15:44,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so, you need to do the breathing, but

201
00:15:47,721 --> 00:15:58,785
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way that clarifies that aspect, because you're breathing all the time, but this aspect is not being discerned.

202
00:15:58,825 --> 00:16:02,527
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do I do the breathing in order to clarify that aspect?

203
00:16:04,167 --> 00:16:09,349
[SPEAKER_01]: If, whenever I do the breathing, it is rooted in hope of the revelation of the aspect?

204
00:16:14,091 --> 00:16:14,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, in the

205
00:16:21,147 --> 00:16:41,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, in that act of breathing, if you don't focus on it too much, having that hope or expectation, like we said, you don't act out of it, this background domain can be discerned.

206
00:16:42,675 --> 00:16:44,436
[SPEAKER_01]: By not focusing too much on the breathing.

207
00:16:44,496 --> 00:16:44,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

208
00:16:46,376 --> 00:16:48,157
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by doing it, but not focusing on it.

209
00:16:48,217 --> 00:16:48,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

210
00:16:50,748 --> 00:16:51,408
[SPEAKER_01]: How's that possible?

211
00:16:52,349 --> 00:16:54,930
[SPEAKER_00]: I can be doing something consciously without focusing on what you're doing.

212
00:16:57,031 --> 00:16:57,972
[SPEAKER_00]: What constitutes too much?

213
00:16:59,753 --> 00:17:05,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you're actively trying to make that the only thing in your experience.

214
00:17:05,296 --> 00:17:12,960
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... it's a... it's an extra effort that people make on top, to kind of isolate this from everything else.

215
00:17:13,080 --> 00:17:14,840
[SPEAKER_00]: And that part is not necessary.

216
00:17:15,501 --> 00:17:16,761
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I'm not isolating it?

217
00:17:17,002 --> 00:17:17,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

218
00:17:18,903 --> 00:17:19,563
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'm just doing it?

219
00:17:23,001 --> 00:17:23,481
[SPEAKER_01]: That's okay.

220
00:17:24,442 --> 00:17:30,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, as long as you keep sight on that motivation, like we said, or that attitude behind it.

221
00:17:30,783 --> 00:17:35,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the attempt to try to isolate it doesn't necessarily arise as an option.

222
00:17:35,965 --> 00:17:39,866
[SPEAKER_00]: It's when those expectations begin to arise and you overlook them.

223
00:17:41,447 --> 00:17:45,308
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that sounds kind of like a... what you're describing sounds like a reference point.

224
00:17:47,888 --> 00:17:49,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Breathing becomes a reference point.

225
00:17:49,689 --> 00:17:49,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

226
00:17:50,463 --> 00:17:56,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Because reference point is obviously not something you directly refer... occupied with all the time.

227
00:17:56,526 --> 00:18:00,307
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just something that's placed, that then things are referred to as they come.

228
00:18:02,508 --> 00:18:02,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

229
00:18:03,069 --> 00:18:10,152
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's exactly why you wouldn't be able to make it an object, because then... Well, I can make it an object, but you're saying I shouldn't.

230
00:18:11,072 --> 00:18:11,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

231
00:18:11,832 --> 00:18:13,493
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you wouldn't be able to do it rightly.

232
00:18:13,793 --> 00:18:14,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, right, right, right.

233
00:18:14,374 --> 00:18:17,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it would cease to be a reference point.

234
00:18:17,095 --> 00:18:19,716
[SPEAKER_01]: If it becomes an object, it ceases to be a reference point.

235
00:18:20,729 --> 00:18:31,216
[SPEAKER_01]: So, breathing as a practice of mindfulness, if it's done as a concentration practice or focusing practice, it ceases to be a reference point, because it becomes an object.

236
00:18:31,256 --> 00:18:31,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

237
00:18:32,777 --> 00:18:43,464
[SPEAKER_01]: And... but if that object, as I said, it's neutral and it provides me with calm, overall, in fact, I'm calmer after I make breathing my object.

238
00:18:45,545 --> 00:18:46,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Could you remind me what the problem with that is?

239
00:18:50,314 --> 00:18:54,918
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a problem if you think that that's clarifying the satipaṭṭhāna of the body.

240
00:18:54,938 --> 00:18:57,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not the body that I'm...

241
00:18:58,420 --> 00:19:05,125
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the actual body that I'm concentrating on and the calm that I experience.

242
00:19:05,245 --> 00:19:08,088
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the calm on account of establishing the satipaṭṭhāna.

243
00:19:08,128 --> 00:19:19,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because just ask yourself, after doing that calming practice, like we said before, if your oxygen were to be cut off, would that practice have made you dispassionate in regard to that possibility?

244
00:19:20,772 --> 00:19:21,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

245
00:19:21,172 --> 00:19:22,033
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't understand it.

246
00:19:22,313 --> 00:19:32,696
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if that calming, focusing practice you did, would that have made you imperturbable in the face of your oxygen being depleted?

247
00:19:33,936 --> 00:19:36,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, the sutta says, the practice of ānāpānasati leads to arahantship.

248
00:19:37,217 --> 00:19:41,399
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it should lead you to dispassion and non-ownership of that body.

249
00:19:42,185 --> 00:19:54,707
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, factually, like, you can do that, but if you think that that's the ānāpānasati that the Buddha taught, which would lead to that result, then, obviously, well, you're missing out on the actual result that you should be looking for.

250
00:19:55,507 --> 00:20:10,530
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the calming that I get on account of making a... breathing a meditation object is not the... well, it's not something that would make me unafraid.

251
00:20:12,158 --> 00:20:12,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Of death?

252
00:20:12,838 --> 00:20:13,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Of death.

253
00:20:14,880 --> 00:20:16,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is the purpose of the practice.

254
00:20:16,580 --> 00:20:19,062
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, that's not ānāpānasati.

255
00:20:19,482 --> 00:20:25,045
[SPEAKER_00]: If your definition of ānāpānasati is that kind of focusing, that has nothing to do with the path anymore.

256
00:20:25,385 --> 00:20:29,367
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not ānāpānasati.

257
00:20:31,028 --> 00:20:37,192
[SPEAKER_01]: But, say, the knowledge of the breath, or the lungs, or the heart,

258
00:20:40,781 --> 00:20:50,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Being possibly cut off at any given point in the future, would that make me unafraid of death?

259
00:20:50,605 --> 00:21:07,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, you can see the direct connection, because as soon as you start thinking about it, you see this... you'll probably see this fear and apprehension coming up, so you can see, oh, this is exactly something that I wouldn't want to think about, something that I wouldn't want to maintain clarity of.

260
00:21:09,577 --> 00:21:17,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it kind of has that jarring aspect to it, that it would be easier just not to think about it, even if it is factually the case.

261
00:21:18,860 --> 00:21:25,061
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, what guarantees a successful thinking about it?

262
00:21:27,522 --> 00:21:28,082
[SPEAKER_00]: You know what I mean?

263
00:21:28,122 --> 00:21:31,963
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, I want to think about it, but yes, it's jarring, it's frightful, it's frightening.

264
00:21:33,204 --> 00:21:38,165
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I make sure I do it rightly, or it doesn't become too much, or...

265
00:21:39,935 --> 00:21:53,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that jarring-ness should never take you to the point where you start acting out and compromising all the prerequisites that allowed you to reveal and see this.

266
00:21:54,262 --> 00:21:59,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Prerequisites such as the precepts... Precepts, sense restraint, not acting out of unwholesome intentions, even mentally.

267
00:22:00,285 --> 00:22:02,946
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I already have been doing that, training in that,

268
00:22:03,790 --> 00:22:18,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, when these jarring pressures come, to run or panic, on account of... well, on account of not being moved by the prospect of death... You don't compromise what you were established in, no matter what.

269
00:22:20,580 --> 00:22:23,522
[SPEAKER_01]: And that would help.

270
00:22:23,722 --> 00:22:24,303
[SPEAKER_01]: How would that help?

271
00:22:24,663 --> 00:22:32,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because... those... the jarringness and all the kind of...

272
00:22:33,674 --> 00:22:46,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, bad things that you might be getting out of it, it's maintained that way, because in the past, whenever those fears would come up, you would try to cover them up somehow.

273
00:22:46,941 --> 00:22:48,702
[SPEAKER_00]: So now, if...

274
00:22:48,722 --> 00:22:51,804
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like the fear gets worse if you run from it.

275
00:22:51,884 --> 00:22:53,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's how you maintain it.

276
00:22:54,185 --> 00:22:55,346
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you stay afraid.

277
00:22:55,446 --> 00:22:55,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

278
00:22:56,847 --> 00:23:01,409
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't run from it, that fear would disappear.

279
00:23:01,633 --> 00:23:05,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that you're... it's like an exposure therapy, so to speak.

280
00:23:08,055 --> 00:23:08,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I suppose, yeah.

281
00:23:08,975 --> 00:23:24,098
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, if the ānāpānasati were to be done like that, then, yeah, indeed, there is a direct connection between not being afraid of death and successful practice of ānāpānasati.

282
00:23:25,459 --> 00:23:31,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you would need to introduce a second step of... get the magical insight of something else, and then you become an arahant.

283
00:23:31,577 --> 00:23:35,479
[SPEAKER_01]: The simple absence of fear would be a proof.

284
00:23:35,659 --> 00:23:35,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

285
00:23:36,980 --> 00:23:42,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Absence of fear in relation to the body breaking apart.

286
00:23:42,283 --> 00:23:44,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, yeah, the most kind of fundamental threat to your existence.

287
00:23:44,864 --> 00:23:45,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

288
00:23:48,827 --> 00:23:50,568
[SPEAKER_01]: And I guess, yeah, that would be calming in and of itself.

289
00:23:51,488 --> 00:23:52,729
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you cannot be afraid.

290
00:23:55,010 --> 00:23:59,913
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, that practice of ānāpānasati, or more broader practice of the mindfulness of the body,

291
00:24:01,378 --> 00:24:22,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Just to recap, would be then on the level of withdrawing yourself from unwholesome through keeping the precepts, restraining from sensuality, coerced by body sensuality, subtler sensuality by speech, mental sensuality acting out of... Hindrances in general.

292
00:24:22,269 --> 00:24:23,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Hindrances in general.

293
00:24:24,810 --> 00:24:27,912
[SPEAKER_01]: So... and then discerning the...

294
00:24:29,644 --> 00:24:34,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Kind of the nature of what's left there, regardless of what you're hoping to come out of it or not.

295
00:24:35,166 --> 00:24:42,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whether I hope or not, that breathing will reveal the body, or the restraint will reveal that which actual body is.

296
00:24:42,529 --> 00:24:44,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether I hope or not, what's there is the body is there.

297
00:24:45,470 --> 00:24:49,231
[SPEAKER_00]: That, well, that which resulted in withdrawal from sensuality is there.

298
00:24:50,032 --> 00:24:52,573
[SPEAKER_01]: We don't even have to call it a body.

299
00:24:53,413 --> 00:24:54,113
[SPEAKER_01]: The breath is there.

300
00:24:55,234 --> 00:24:59,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether I choose to do it or not, choose to overdo it or not, underdo it or not,

301
00:25:00,407 --> 00:25:01,167
[SPEAKER_01]: The body is breathing.

302
00:25:01,988 --> 00:25:05,270
[SPEAKER_00]: And the threat to its continuation is also there, at the same time.

303
00:25:05,530 --> 00:25:10,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so you can always connect with it on the right level, so to speak, indirectly.

304
00:25:12,113 --> 00:25:15,255
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're, yeah, not covering it up with all these other things.

305
00:25:15,715 --> 00:25:23,099
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but what if I'm doing these practices and there is no fear coming up?

306
00:25:23,139 --> 00:25:24,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I doing something wrong or...?

307
00:25:26,795 --> 00:25:27,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Not necessarily.

308
00:25:28,076 --> 00:25:47,833
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are sure, perfectly sure, that you haven't been doing all the... if all these prerequisites of your behavior are well established, then, if fear doesn't come up, you don't have to then... like, are you going to become afraid of that?

309
00:25:48,293 --> 00:25:52,176
[SPEAKER_00]: That fear isn't coming up and start, like, trying to get get it back going.

310
00:25:52,757 --> 00:25:53,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you're

311
00:25:54,208 --> 00:25:57,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Putting your head into the situation, whereas the whole point is to...

312
00:25:57,150 --> 00:26:10,337
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the fear is not coming up, and I recognize, okay, I'm not panicking on account of that, or feeling that I must invoke the fear, then, does that, like, protecting that apply to this?

313
00:26:10,357 --> 00:26:11,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you just protect it.

314
00:26:12,338 --> 00:26:14,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and then the fear might... See what comes up.

315
00:26:14,219 --> 00:26:20,723
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because then... See, even in that moment where you realize, oh, the fear isn't coming up,

316
00:26:21,565 --> 00:26:28,302
[SPEAKER_00]: You might realize that's moving you now, like you might... those same expectations we were talking about before.

317
00:26:29,075 --> 00:26:31,637
[SPEAKER_00]: That would pollute the entire practice.

318
00:26:32,058 --> 00:26:41,906
[SPEAKER_00]: We'll be coming up right then and there, because you expect to accelerate by getting more fear, more this, more that, but then, that's exactly what we said before, we'd be covering up the body.

319
00:26:43,067 --> 00:26:45,509
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to... contain that.

320
00:26:45,549 --> 00:26:51,174
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, regardless of how it feels, the point of it is not to get a specific experience out of it.

321
00:26:52,235 --> 00:26:55,418
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is to maintain that perspective, regardless of whatever comes up.

322
00:26:58,167 --> 00:26:58,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

323
00:26:59,208 --> 00:27:04,512
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about a specific experience, regardless of the hope, but maintaining the whole setup.

324
00:27:04,712 --> 00:27:04,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

325
00:27:06,193 --> 00:27:07,114
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's gonna feel...

326
00:27:07,674 --> 00:27:09,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if there is a specific experience... Yeah.

327
00:27:11,737 --> 00:27:14,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there might be, and there actually probably will be, to some extent.

328
00:27:14,640 --> 00:27:14,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

329
00:27:15,280 --> 00:27:17,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Some degree of novelty, some degree of revelations or insight.

330
00:27:19,623 --> 00:27:20,985
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not what I should emphasize.

331
00:27:21,745 --> 00:27:24,167
[SPEAKER_01]: You will fall from the practice if you start emphasizing that.

